# 1g/implfor — a spec of THIS module, supplied for a type from another.
#
# GRAMMAR#impl-decl's target is a `type` and GRAMMAR#base-type derives a dotted name, so
# `impl Show for shape.P` is a form — and it is the ordinary way to make a foreign type
# satisfy an interface you own. The orphan rule allows exactly this: an impl belongs to the
# spec's module or the type's, and here it is the spec's.
#
# THE NAME IT RESOLVES TO IS THE BARE ONE. Every module of a program flattens into one
# namespace, and two modules declaring one type name is refused (E3078) — so `shape.P` is `P`,
# uniquely. The qualifier is a separate claim, about which module it came from, and it joins
# the list every other type position's qualifier joins: an invented one is `undefined name`.
#
# The SEED builds this and runs it, which is worth saying: it drops the qualifier and reads the
# bare name, and for a program whose qualifier is TRUE that is the same answer. What it cannot
# do is the other half — `impl Show for bogus.P` builds there too, which is its gap and not this
# file's business.
import "./shape"

spec Show {
	fn show() -> int
}

impl Show for shape.P {
	fn show() -> int {
		return this.x * 2
	}
}

fn main() {
	p := shape.P(21)
	raise "impl on an imported type" if p.show() != 42

	print "implfor: ok"
}
